# The Semgrep job's tool.
#
# pip-compile turns this into semgrep.txt: every package, exact versions AND hashes, installed with
# `--require-hashes`. Dependabot keeps it current by re-running pip-compile on the Python its
# header names (3.11), so a bump never pins a set that cannot be installed together. Edit
# THIS file, then regenerate (run in python:3.11-slim):
#   pip-compile --generate-hashes --no-annotate --strip-extras --unsafe-package=pyjwt \
#     --output-file=semgrep.txt semgrep.in
#
# PyJWT is left out of semgrep.txt (--unsafe-package) and pinned on its own in semgrep-pyjwt.txt.
# semgrep 1.178.0 declares pyjwt~=2.13.0, and every 2.13 release is vulnerable (GHSA-ffc3-869f-jxw9
# and ten more; fixed in 2.15.0), so no set that honours semgrep's pin is safe. The job installs
# both files with --no-deps: the two together are the whole closure, hash-checked, and semgrep scan
# runs on PyJWT 2.15 (it imports jwt at startup for its MCP server, and never verifies a token in a
# scan). Dependabot keeps --unsafe-package (it reads it back from the header) and updates
# semgrep-pyjwt.txt on its own. Once a semgrep release allows a safe PyJWT, drop the option and the
# second file and install semgrep.txt alone again.
semgrep
